    "thm-rademacher-contraction-inequality": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-one-sided-empirical-rademacher-complexity",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $m\\ge1$, let $S=(z_1,\\ldots,z_m)$ be a fixed sample in a measurable space $\\mathcal X$, and let $\\mathcal F$ be a nonempty finite or countable class of measurable real functions such that the displayed supremum is finite and measurable on the finite sign space. Let $\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_m$ be independent uniform signs. The **one-sided empirical Rademacher complexity** is\n\n$$\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal F):=\\frac1m\\,\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\sup_{f\\in\\mathcal F}\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sigma_i f(z_i).$$\n\nThere is no absolute value in this definition and $0$ need not belong to $\\mathcal F$. It differs from the absolute-value complexity $m^{-1}\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\sup_{f\\in\\mathcal F}|\\sum_i\\sigma_i f(z_i)|$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-one-sided-coordinate-rademacher-contraction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $m\\ge1$, let $z_1,\\ldots,z_m$ be fixed, and let $\\mathcal F$ be a nonempty finite class of real-valued functions on their domain. For each $i$, let $\\Phi_i:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ satisfy $|\\Phi_i(x)-\\Phi_i(y)|\\le L|x-y|$ for all $x,y\\in\\mathbb R$, with a common $L\\ge0$. Then\n\n$$\\frac1m\\,\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\sup_{f\\in\\mathcal F}\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sigma_i\\Phi_i(f(z_i))\\le L\\,\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal F).$$\n\nHere $S=(z_1,\\ldots,z_m)$ and $\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal F):=m^{-1}2^{-m}\\sum_{\\sigma\\in\\{-1,1\\}^m}\\sup_{f\\in\\mathcal F}\\sum_i\\sigma_i f(z_i)$. Expectations over signs mean this uniform finite average; no measurability on the functions' domain is needed. For measurable classes on a measurable space this agrees with [[def-one-sided-empirical-rademacher-complexity]]. Equivalently, the unnormalized left expectation is at most $L$ times the unnormalized one-sided supremum on the right.\n\nThe same holds for a countable class when both suprema are finite for every sign vector. No condition $\\Phi_i(0)=0$ is required; additive constants cancel because the proof compares coordinate differences.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "If $\\mathcal H$ is finite or countable, [F1] identifies the right-hand side with the published supplier's one-sided normalization, and [F2] gives the inequality with exactly the stated common Lipschitz constant and no zero-at-zero assumption.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Otherwise, use the specified countable subclass $\\mathcal H_0$. For every sign vector, its transformed and untransformed suprema equal those over $\\mathcal H$ by hypothesis; averaging over the finite sign space therefore gives the same two quantities. Applying step 1.1 to $\\mathcal H_0$ proves the displayed inequality for $\\mathcal H$.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
