    "thm-rademacher-symmetrization-bound": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-rademacher-complexity",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathcal Z,\\mathcal A,P)$ be a probability space and let $\\mathcal G$ be a nonempty class of $\\mathcal A$-measurable functions $g:\\mathcal Z\\to[0,1]$. For a fixed sample $S=(z_1,\\ldots,z_m)\\in\\mathcal Z^m$, where $m\\ge1$, let $\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_m$ be independent uniform signs in $\\{-1,+1\\}$. Define\n\n$$P_mg:=\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m g(z_i),\\qquad Pg:=\\int g\\,dP,$$\n\nand define the empirical Rademacher complexity by\n\n$$\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G):=\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\left[\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sigma_i g(z_i)\\right].$$\n\nHere $\\mathbb E_\\sigma$ is the uniform average over the $2^m$ sign vectors. If $S=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_m)$ is an IID sample with common law $P$, and the displayed supremum is measurable as a function of $S$, the expected Rademacher complexity is\n\n$$\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G):=\\mathbb E_{S\\sim P^m}\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G).$$\n\nThe class may be finite, countable, or uncountable when the indicated supremum is measurable; a specified countable subclass giving the same supremum for every sign vector for almost every sample suffices. This is the one-sided convention: the definition contains no absolute value, and zero need not belong to $\\mathcal G$. Both complexities lie in $[0,1]$: every signed average lies in $[-1,1]$, and for any fixed $g_0\\in\\mathcal G$ the sign-average supremum is at least the sign-average for $g_0$, which is zero.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-identically-distributed-and-iid-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ be random elements with the same measurable target $(E,\\mathcal E)$. They are **identically distributed** if $\\mathbb P(X_i\\in B)=\\mathbb P(X_j\\in B)$ for all $i,j\\in I$ and $B\\in\\mathcal E$, that is, their laws in [[def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element]] agree. They are **independent and identically distributed (IID)** if, in addition, the whole family is independent in [[def-independent-random-elements]]. Independence means mutual independence, not merely pairwise independence. No moment assumption is part of either definition. The empty family satisfies these universal conditions vacuously.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-independent-random-elements-have-product-joint-law",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, and let\n$X_i:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S_i,\\Sigma_i)$ for $i<n$ be independent\nrandom elements. Define\n\n$$X=(X_0,\\dots,X_{n-1}):\\Omega\\to\\prod_{i<n}S_i.$$\n\nThen $X$ is a random element of\n$\\left(\\prod_{i<n}S_i,\\bigotimes_{i<n}\\Sigma_i\\right)$, and its law is the\nfinite product of the marginal laws:\n\n$$\\mathbb P_X=\\bigotimes_{i<n}\\mathbb P_{X_i}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-change-of-variables-for-expectation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S,\\Sigma)$ be a random element, let\n$\\mathbb P_X$ be its law, and let $g:(S,\\Sigma)\\to\\mathbb R$ or\n$g:(S,\\Sigma)\\to\\mathbb C$ be measurable.\n\n1. If $g\\ge0$, then\n   $$\\mathbb E[g(X)]=\\int_S g\\,d\\mathbb P_X.$$\n2. If $g(X)$ is integrable, then $g$ is integrable with respect to\n   $\\mathbb P_X$ and the same formula holds:\n   $$\\mathbb E[g(X)]=\\int_S g\\,d\\mathbb P_X.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces. Then:\n\n1. the set function $E \\mapsto (\\mu \\times \\nu)(E)$ of\n   [[def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces]] is a measure on\n   $\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B$;\n2. for measurable rectangles,\n   $$(\\mu \\times \\nu)(A \\times B)=\\mu(A)\\nu(B),$$\n   with the nonnegative extended-product convention $0\\cdot\\infty=\\infty\\cdot0=0$;\n3. the measure $\\mu \\times \\nu$ is sigma-finite; and\n4. it is the unique measure on $\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B$ with the\n   rectangle formula.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to \\mathbb C$ belong to\n$L^1(\\mu \\times \\nu)$. Then:\n\n1. for $\\mu$-almost every $x$, the section $f_x$ belongs to $L^1(\\nu)$;\n2. for $\\nu$-almost every $y$, the section $f^y$ belongs to $L^1(\\mu)$;\n3. after assigning the value $0$ on the exceptional parameter sets, the\n   section-integral functions are integrable; and\n4. the three integrals agree:\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "For each fixed sample $S$ and each $g\\in\\mathcal G$, [F2]–[F3] give $\\mathbb E_{S'}P_m'g=Pg$, so $Pg-P_mg=\\mathbb E_{S'}(P_m'g-P_mg)$. For any bounded family $X_g$, $\\sup_g\\mathbb E X_g\\le\\mathbb E\\sup_gX_g$, since each $\\mathbb E X_g$ is at most $\\mathbb E\\sup_hX_h$. Applying this conditionally on $S$ and integrating, with [F5] to identify the iterated and joint product expectations, gives $\\mathbb E_S\\sup_g(Pg-P_mg)\\le\\mathbb E_{S,S'}\\sup_g(P_m'g-P_mg)$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "By [F2], the $2m$ coordinates of $(S,S')$ have product law $P^{2m}$. For each fixed sign vector, swap $Z_i$ and $Z_i'$ exactly when $\\sigma_i=-1$. This coordinate permutation preserves the product law by [F4] and sends each difference $g(Z_i')-g(Z_i)$ to $\\sigma_i(g(Z_i')-g(Z_i))$. Averaging over the finite sign space gives $\\mathbb E_{S,S'}\\sup_g(P_m'g-P_mg)=\\mathbb E_{S,S',\\sigma}\\sup_g m^{-1}\\sum_i\\sigma_i(g(Z_i')-g(Z_i))$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-3.1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "For every pair of samples and sign vector, subadditivity gives $\\sup_g m^{-1}\\sum_i\\sigma_i(g(Z_i')-g(Z_i))\\le\\sup_g m^{-1}\\sum_i\\sigma_i g(Z_i')+\\sup_g m^{-1}\\sum_i(-\\sigma_i)g(Z_i)$. The first term has expectation $\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G)$ by [F1] and the law of $S'$; the second has the same expectation because $-\\sigma$ is uniform on the sign cube and $S$ has law $P^m$ by [F2]. Combining with steps 1.1–2.1 proves the result.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
