    "thm-rademacher-uniform-generalization": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-rademacher-complexity",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathcal Z,\\mathcal A,P)$ be a probability space and let $\\mathcal G$ be a nonempty class of $\\mathcal A$-measurable functions $g:\\mathcal Z\\to[0,1]$. For a fixed sample $S=(z_1,\\ldots,z_m)\\in\\mathcal Z^m$, where $m\\ge1$, let $\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_m$ be independent uniform signs in $\\{-1,+1\\}$. Define\n\n$$P_mg:=\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m g(z_i),\\qquad Pg:=\\int g\\,dP,$$\n\nand define the empirical Rademacher complexity by\n\n$$\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G):=\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\left[\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sigma_i g(z_i)\\right].$$\n\nHere $\\mathbb E_\\sigma$ is the uniform average over the $2^m$ sign vectors. If $S=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_m)$ is an IID sample with common law $P$, and the displayed supremum is measurable as a function of $S$, the expected Rademacher complexity is\n\n$$\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G):=\\mathbb E_{S\\sim P^m}\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G).$$\n\nThe class may be finite, countable, or uncountable when the indicated supremum is measurable; a specified countable subclass giving the same supremum for every sign vector for almost every sample suffices. This is the one-sided convention: the definition contains no absolute value, and zero need not belong to $\\mathcal G$. Both complexities lie in $[0,1]$: every signed average lies in $[-1,1]$, and for any fixed $g_0\\in\\mathcal G$ the sign-average supremum is at least the sign-average for $g_0$, which is zero.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-rademacher-symmetrization-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathcal Z,\\mathcal A,P)$ be a probability space, let $m\\ge1$, and let $\\mathcal G$ be a nonempty class of measurable functions $g:\\mathcal Z\\to[0,1]$. Let $S=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_m)$ be IID with law $P$, let $S'=(Z_1',\\ldots,Z_m')$ be an independent IID copy on a finite product probability extension, and let $\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_m$ be independent uniform signs independent of both samples. Write $P_mg=m^{-1}\\sum_i g(Z_i)$ and $P_m'g=m^{-1}\\sum_i g(Z_i')$. Assume the random suprema $\\sup_g(Pg-P_mg)$, $\\sup_g(P_m'g-P_mg)$, $\\sup_g\\sum_i\\sigma_i(g(Z_i')-g(Z_i))$, and $\\sup_g\\sum_i\\sigma_i g(Z_i)$ are measurable on their respective product sigma algebras. Then, with $\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G)$ the expected one-sided Rademacher complexity,\n\n$$\\mathbb E_S\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}(Pg-P_mg)\\le2\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G).$$\n\nThis is a one-sided expectation inequality; it does not assert a tail bound or an absolute-value deviation bound. No choice axiom is assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-mcdiarmid-bounded-differences",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $n\\ge1$, let $Z_i:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S_i,\\Sigma_i)$ be independent random elements, meaning measurable maps ([[def-random-element-and-real-random-variable]]), with laws $\\mu_i$ given by pushforward ([[def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element]]). Put $\\mu=\\bigotimes_{i=1}^n\\mu_i$ on the finite product sigma-algebra ([[def-product-sigma-algebra-and-finite-product-sigma-algebras]]). Let $f:\\prod_{i=1}^nS_i\\to\\mathbb R$ be product-measurable with $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu<\\infty$. Suppose finite deterministic constants $c_i\\ge0$ satisfy\n$$|f(z_1,\\ldots,z_i,\\ldots,z_n)-f(z_1,\\ldots,z_i',\\ldots,z_n)|\\le c_i$$\nfor every fixed setting of the other coordinates and every $z_i,z_i'$. The independent product-law theorem identifies the law of $Z=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_n)$ as $\\mu$. Applying change of variables for expectation first to $|f|$ and then to $f$ makes $f(Z)$ integrable and gives $\\mathbb E f(Z)=\\int f\\,d\\mu$. Put $V=\\sum_{i=1}^n c_i^2$. For $t>0$, if $V>0$ then\n$$\\mathbb P\\bigl(f(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_n)-\\mathbb E f(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_n)\\ge t\\bigr)\\le\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{2t^2}{V}\\right),$$\nand if $V=0$ this probability is $0$. The same bound holds for the lower tail, so the two-sided probability is at most $2e^{-2t^2/V}$ when $V>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-identically-distributed-and-iid-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ be random elements with the same measurable target $(E,\\mathcal E)$. They are **identically distributed** if $\\mathbb P(X_i\\in B)=\\mathbb P(X_j\\in B)$ for all $i,j\\in I$ and $B\\in\\mathcal E$, that is, their laws in [[def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element]] agree. They are **independent and identically distributed (IID)** if, in addition, the whole family is independent in [[def-independent-random-elements]]. Independence means mutual independence, not merely pairwise independence. No moment assumption is part of either definition. The empty family satisfies these universal conditions vacuously.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-probability-measure-basic-identities",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$ be a probability space, let $A,B\\in\\mathcal F$,\nand let $(A_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb N}$ be events.\n\n1. $\\mathbb P(A^c)=1-\\mathbb P(A)$.\n2. If $A\\subseteq B$, then $\\mathbb P(A)\\le\\mathbb P(B)$.\n3. If $A\\subseteq B$, then $\\mathbb P(B\\setminus A)=\\mathbb P(B)-\\mathbb P(A)$.\n4. $\\mathbb P(\\bigcup_n A_n)\\le\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\mathbb P(A_n)$, and for every\n   natural $m$,\n   $$\\mathbb P\\left(\\bigcup_{n<m}A_n\\right)\\le\\sum_{n<m}\\mathbb P(A_n).$$\n5. $\\mathbb P(A\\cup B)=\\mathbb P(A)+\\mathbb P(B)-\\mathbb P(A\\cap B)$.\n6. If $A_n\\uparrow A$, then\n   $$\\mathbb P(A)=\\sup_n\\mathbb P(A_n).$$\n   If $A_n\\downarrow A$, then\n   $$\\mathbb P(A)=\\inf_n\\mathbb P(A_n).$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.2",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Put $\\Psi(S):=\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}(Pg-P_mg)$. Changing one coordinate changes each term in this supremum by at most $1/m$, hence $|\\Psi(S)-\\Psi(S')|\\le1/m$. By the Statement, $\\Psi$ is measurable, and it is bounded because $0\\le g\\le1$. Since $0<\\delta<1$, [F5] and $m\\ge1$ give $t:=\\sqrt{\\log(1/\\delta)/(2m)}>0$; [F4]–[F5] give $\\exp(-2mt^2)=\\delta$. McDiarmid [F3] with $c_i=1/m$ gives $\\Psi(S)\\le\\mathbb E\\Psi(S)+t$ with probability at least $1-\\delta$, and [F2] bounds its expectation by $2\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G)$. This proves the first one-sided bound.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "For each fixed sample, $\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\overline{\\mathcal G})=\\mathbb E_\\sigma[m^{-1}\\sum_i\\sigma_i+\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}m^{-1}\\sum_i(-\\sigma_i)g(Z_i)]$. The first term has mean zero and $-\\sigma$ has the same uniform law as $\\sigma$, so this equals $\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)$; taking expectations gives $\\mathfrak R_m(\\overline{\\mathcal G})=\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G)$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "For each fixed sign vector, changing one observation changes every $m^{-1}\\sum_i\\sigma_i g(Z_i)$ by at most $1/m$, since $g$ takes values in $[0,1]$; the same bound holds for the supremum and then for its sign average $\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)$. This statistic is bounded and measurable by the Statement. With $t=\\sqrt{\\log(1/\\delta)/(2m)}>0$ as in step 1.1, [F3] applied to its negative gives $\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G)\\le\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)+t$ with probability at least $1-\\delta$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.2",
          "step": "2.2",
          "claim": "For $h=1-g$, $Ph-P_mh=P_mg-Pg$. Apply step 1.1 to $\\mathcal G$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal G}$ with failure probability $\\delta/2$ each. By [F7] and step 1.2, both deviations are at most $2\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G)+\\sqrt{\\log(2/\\delta)/(2m)}$ except on an event of probability at most $\\delta$. This proves the first two-sided bound.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F7",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-3.1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Apply step 1.1 with failure probability $\\delta/2$ and step 2.1 with failure probability $\\delta/2$. By [F7], both events hold with probability at least $1-\\delta$. Since $2/\\delta>1$, [F5] gives $t:=\\sqrt{\\log(2/\\delta)/(2m)}>0$; combining the two bounds gives $\\Psi(S)\\le2\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G)+t\\le2\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)+3t$. This is the second one-sided guarantee.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F7",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-4.1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "Apply step 3.1 to $\\mathcal G$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal G}$ with failure probability $\\delta/2$ each. Their complexities agree by step 1.2, and their upper deviations are $\\sup_g(Pg-P_mg)$ and $\\sup_g(P_mg-Pg)$, respectively. By [F7] the two bounds hold together with probability at least $1-\\delta$, giving the last two-sided bound with $\\log(4/\\delta)$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F7",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
