    "cor-erm-excess-risk-from-uniform-deviation": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-supervised-learning-problem",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **supervised prediction problem** consists of nonempty measurable spaces $(\\mathsf X,\\Sigma_X)$ of instances, $(\\mathsf Y,\\Sigma_Y)$ of labels, and $(\\mathsf A,\\Sigma_A)$ of prediction actions; the product measurable space $(\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Y,\\Sigma_X\\otimes\\Sigma_Y)$; a probability measure $P$ on that product; a nonempty class $\\mathcal F$ of measurable predictors $f:\\mathsf X\\to\\mathsf A$; and a loss $\\ell:\\mathsf A\\times\\mathsf Y\\to[0,+\\infty]$ that is measurable with respect to $\\Sigma_A\\otimes\\Sigma_Y$. The law $P$ need not be supported on a deterministic labeling function, and this definition imposes no assumption that sample observations be independent or share a common law.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-population-and-empirical-risk",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a supervised prediction problem with joint law $P$, the **population risk** of any measurable predictor $f:\\mathsf X\\to\\mathsf A$ (in particular, every $f\\in\\mathcal F$) is the extended nonnegative integral\n$$R_P(f):=\\int_{\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Y}\\ell(f(x),y)\\,P(d(x,y))\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nFor an ordered sample $S=((x_1,y_1),\\ldots,(x_m,y_m))$ of length $m\\ge1$, the **empirical risk** or **training risk** of any such $f$ is\n$$\\widehat R_S(f):=\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\ell(f(x_i),y_i)\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nNeither definition assumes finite risk. The sum uses extended nonnegative arithmetic; since $m\\ge1$, any summand equal to $+\\infty$ makes the empirical risk $+\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-empirical-risk-minimizer",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a sample $S$ and a nonempty class $\\mathcal F$ of predictors. Its **empirical minimum** is $\\widehat R_S^*(\\mathcal F):=\\inf_{f\\in\\mathcal F}\\widehat R_S(f)\\in[0,+\\infty]$. An **exact empirical risk minimizer** is a predictor $f\\in\\mathcal F$ with $\\widehat R_S(f)=\\widehat R_S^*(\\mathcal F)$. If the infimum is finite but is not attained, no exact minimizer exists. If the infimum is $+\\infty$, nonemptiness implies that every member has infinite empirical risk and is an exact minimizer.\n\nFor finite $\\eta\\ge0$, a predictor $f\\in\\mathcal F$ is an **$\\eta$-approximate empirical risk minimizer** if either the empirical minimum is $+\\infty$ and $f$ is an exact minimizer, or the empirical minimum is finite and $f$ has finite empirical risk at most $\\widehat R_S^*(\\mathcal F)+\\eta$. The membership requirement $f\\in\\mathcal F$ applies in both branches. Thus the definition never forms $+\\infty+\\eta$. When $\\eta=0$ and the finite infimum is not attained, no such predictor exists.\n\nFor a fixed sample size $m$, a **measurable prediction rule** with class $\\mathcal F$ is a jointly measurable evaluation map from the sample space $((\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Y)^m, (\\Sigma_X\\otimes\\Sigma_Y)^{\\otimes m})$ and a new instance in $\\mathsf X$ to $\\mathsf A$, whose slice at each sample belongs to $\\mathcal F$. It is an exact or approximate ERM rule when every slice satisfies the corresponding samplewise definition above. This definition asserts no existence of a measurable selector for an arbitrary class.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-population-and-empirical-risk-are-measurable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In a supervised prediction problem, for every measurable predictor $f$, the function $(x,y)\\mapsto\\ell(f(x),y)$ is measurable, so its population risk is a defined extended nonnegative integral. For every sample size $m\\ge1$, the empirical risk $S\\mapsto\\widehat R_S(f)$ is an extended nonnegative measurable function on the finite product sample space.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-rademacher-uniform-generalization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice (AC). Let $(\\mathcal Z,\\mathcal A,P)$ be a probability space, let $m\\ge1$, and let $\\mathcal G$ be a nonempty class of measurable functions $g:\\mathcal Z\\to[0,1]$. Take $S=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_m)$ to be the coordinate sample with law $P^m$. Put $P_mg=m^{-1}\\sum_i g(Z_i)$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal G}:=\\{1-g:g\\in\\mathcal G\\}$. Assume the sample, independent-copy and sign suprema over $\\mathcal G$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal G}$ used in the displayed claims and the symmetrization argument are measurable. Let $\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_m$ be independent uniform signs, and let\n\n$$\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G):=\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sigma_i g(Z_i),\\qquad \\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G):=\\mathbb E_{S\\sim P^m}\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G),$$\n\nwhere the $\\sigma_i$ are independent uniform signs, and let $\\delta\\in(0,1)$. Each of the following four guarantees holds with probability at least $1-\\delta$ over $S$:\n\n$$\\forall g\\in\\mathcal G,\\qquad Pg\\le P_mg+2\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G)+\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(1/\\delta)}{2m}},$$\n\n$$\\forall g\\in\\mathcal G,\\qquad Pg\\le P_mg+2\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)+3\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(2/\\delta)}{2m}},$$\n\n$$\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}|Pg-P_mg|\\le2\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G)+\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(2/\\delta)}{2m}},$$\n\n$$\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}|Pg-P_mg|\\le2\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)+3\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(4/\\delta)}{2m}}.$$\n\nThe four guarantees are separate probability statements. The choice axiom is used through the bounded-differences theorem.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-rademacher-uniform-generalization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice (AC). Let $(\\mathcal Z,\\mathcal A,P)$ be a probability space, let $m\\ge1$, and let $\\mathcal G$ be a nonempty class of measurable functions $g:\\mathcal Z\\to[0,1]$. Take $S=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_m)$ to be the coordinate sample with law $P^m$. Put $P_mg=m^{-1}\\sum_i g(Z_i)$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal G}:=\\{1-g:g\\in\\mathcal G\\}$. Assume the sample, independent-copy and sign suprema over $\\mathcal G$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal G}$ used in the displayed claims and the symmetrization argument are measurable. Let $\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_m$ be independent uniform signs, and let\n\n$$\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G):=\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sigma_i g(Z_i),\\qquad \\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G):=\\mathbb E_{S\\sim P^m}\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G),$$\n\nwhere the $\\sigma_i$ are independent uniform signs, and let $\\delta\\in(0,1)$. Each of the following four guarantees holds with probability at least $1-\\delta$ over $S$:\n\n$$\\forall g\\in\\mathcal G,\\qquad Pg\\le P_mg+2\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G)+\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(1/\\delta)}{2m}},$$\n\n$$\\forall g\\in\\mathcal G,\\qquad Pg\\le P_mg+2\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)+3\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(2/\\delta)}{2m}},$$\n\n$$\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}|Pg-P_mg|\\le2\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G)+\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(2/\\delta)}{2m}},$$\n\n$$\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}|Pg-P_mg|\\le2\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)+3\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(4/\\delta)}{2m}}.$$\n\nThe four guarantees are separate probability statements. The choice axiom is used through the bounded-differences theorem.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-identically-distributed-and-iid-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ be random elements with the same measurable target $(E,\\mathcal E)$. They are **identically distributed** if $\\mathbb P(X_i\\in B)=\\mathbb P(X_j\\in B)$ for all $i,j\\in I$ and $B\\in\\mathcal E$, that is, their laws in [[def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element]] agree. They are **independent and identically distributed (IID)** if, in addition, the whole family is independent in [[def-independent-random-elements]]. Independence means mutual independence, not merely pairwise independence. No moment assumption is part of either definition. The empty family satisfies these universal conditions vacuously.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "For each $h\\in\\mathcal H$, the supervised loss function $g_h$ is measurable by [F1] and [F4]. By [F2], $R_P(h)=Pg_h$ and $\\widehat R_S(h)=P_mg_h$, so the uniform bounds in [F5]–[F6] control all predictors in $\\mathcal H$ under the IID hypothesis [F7].",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "On the event in [F5], put $d:=2\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G)+\\sqrt{\\log(2/\\delta)/(2m)}$. Then every $h$ satisfies $R_P(h)\\le\\widehat R_S(h)+d$ and $\\widehat R_S(h)\\le R_P(h)+d$. By [F3], $\\widehat R_S(\\widehat h_\\eta)\\le\\inf_h\\widehat R_S(h)+\\eta$. Since $\\widehat h_\\eta(S)\\in\\mathcal H$ by [F3], applying the first inequality to $\\widehat h_\\eta$ and taking the infimum in the second gives $R_P(\\widehat h_\\eta)-\\inf_hR_P(h)\\le d+\\inf_h\\widehat R_S(h)+\\eta-\\inf_hR_P(h)\\le2d+\\eta$, where $\\inf_h\\widehat R_S(h)\\le\\inf_hR_P(h)+d$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.2",
          "step": "2.2",
          "claim": "On the event in [F6], put $d:=2\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)+3\\sqrt{\\log(4/\\delta)/(2m)}$. The two inequalities $R_P(h)\\le\\widehat R_S(h)+d$ and $\\widehat R_S(h)\\le R_P(h)+d$ hold for every $h$. Since $\\widehat h_\\eta(S)\\in\\mathcal H$, [F3] permits applying the first inequality to the selected predictor and gives $R_P(\\widehat h_\\eta)-\\inf_hR_P(h)\\le d+\\inf_h\\widehat R_S(h)+\\eta-\\inf_hR_P(h)\\le2d+\\eta$, using $\\inf_h\\widehat R_S(h)\\le\\inf_hR_P(h)+d$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-3.1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "The events in [F5] and [F6] are the AC-dependent concentration branch [A1]. Each has probability at least $1-\\delta$; step 2.1 proves the first displayed excess bound on the [F5] event, and step 2.2 proves the second on the [F6] event. Substituting each value of $d$ from those steps gives respectively $4\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G)+2\\sqrt{\\log(2/\\delta)/(2m)}+\\eta$ and $4\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)+6\\sqrt{\\log(4/\\delta)/(2m)}+\\eta$. The guarantees are separate.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
