      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "alpha-batch-5",
        "notes": "Read entire Statement, A1/F1-F7, Proof1.1/2.1/2.2/3.1 and nine direct supplier interfaces. Opened current batch2 supervised-problem, risk and ERM Definitions and complete measurable-risk lemma proof. Bounded [0,1] loss makes risks and infima finite; induced g_h is product-measurable, predictor class nonempty. Existing jointly measurable rule and its samplewise membership/eta inequality are hypotheses; selected population-risk measurability is separately assumed, so no arbitrary-class measurable selection or parameter integration theorem is inferred. IID joint sample law fits coordinate-product uniform theorem. On either uniform event all predictors including selected h satisfy the same deviation; infimum comparison needs no attained population minimizer or selector, yielding 2d+eta and constants4/2 and4/6. AC inherited from concentration only. Checked eta=0 conditional on rule existence, nonattained infima for eta>0, singleton/constant class, m=1 and 0<delta<1; guarantees remain separate. Mohri Theorem3.3 and B&M Theorem8 furnish uniform-bound context, ERM comparison proved locally. No diagram, iff, missing prerequisite or consumer repair."
      }
    },
    "ex-finite-class-rademacher-complexity": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-rademacher-complexity",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathcal Z,\\mathcal A,P)$ be a probability space and let $\\mathcal G$ be a nonempty class of $\\mathcal A$-measurable functions $g:\\mathcal Z\\to[0,1]$. For a fixed sample $S=(z_1,\\ldots,z_m)\\in\\mathcal Z^m$, where $m\\ge1$, let $\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_m$ be independent uniform signs in $\\{-1,+1\\}$. Define\n\n$$P_mg:=\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m g(z_i),\\qquad Pg:=\\int g\\,dP,$$\n\nand define the empirical Rademacher complexity by\n\n$$\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G):=\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\left[\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sigma_i g(z_i)\\right].$$\n\nHere $\\mathbb E_\\sigma$ is the uniform average over the $2^m$ sign vectors. If $S=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_m)$ is an IID sample with common law $P$, and the displayed supremum is measurable as a function of $S$, the expected Rademacher complexity is\n\n$$\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G):=\\mathbb E_{S\\sim P^m}\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G).$$\n\nThe class may be finite, countable, or uncountable when the indicated supremum is measurable; a specified countable subclass giving the same supremum for every sign vector for almost every sample suffices. This is the one-sided convention: the definition contains no absolute value, and zero need not belong to $\\mathcal G$. Both complexities lie in $[0,1]$: every signed average lies in $[-1,1]$, and for any fixed $g_0\\in\\mathcal G$ the sign-average supremum is at least the sign-average for $g_0$, which is zero.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "If $Z_1=a$, then for sign $\\sigma_1=1$ the supremum of $\\sigma_1g(Z_1)$ over $\\mathcal G$ is $1$, and for $\\sigma_1=-1$ it is $0$. Thus $\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)=\\tfrac12(1+0)=\\tfrac12$. If $Z_1=b$, both functions vanish at the observation, so $\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)=0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "The two sample outcomes have probabilities $p$ and $1-p$. Averaging the values from step 1.1 gives $\\mathfrak R_1(\\mathcal G)=p(\\tfrac12)+(1-p)0=p/2$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
