    "ex-bounded-linear-predictor-rademacher-bound": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-cauchy-schwarz-for-real-and-complex-inner-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For vectors $u,v$ in a real or complex inner product space,\n\n$$|\\langle u,v\\rangle|\\le\\lVert u\\rVert\\lVert v\\rVert.$$\n\nEquality holds if and only if $u$ and $v$ are linearly dependent, including the case in which either vector is zero.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-cauchy-schwarz-for-finite-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For real random variables $X,Y$ on a finite probability space,\n$$\\mathbb E[XY]^2\\le\\mathbb E[X^2]\\mathbb E[Y^2].$$\nNo equality characterization is asserted on outcomes of probability zero.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-rademacher-complexity",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathcal Z,\\mathcal A,P)$ be a probability space and let $\\mathcal G$ be a nonempty class of $\\mathcal A$-measurable functions $g:\\mathcal Z\\to[0,1]$. For a fixed sample $S=(z_1,\\ldots,z_m)\\in\\mathcal Z^m$, where $m\\ge1$, let $\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_m$ be independent uniform signs in $\\{-1,+1\\}$. Define\n\n$$P_mg:=\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m g(z_i),\\qquad Pg:=\\int g\\,dP,$$\n\nand define the empirical Rademacher complexity by\n\n$$\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G):=\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\left[\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sigma_i g(z_i)\\right].$$\n\nHere $\\mathbb E_\\sigma$ is the uniform average over the $2^m$ sign vectors. If $S=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_m)$ is an IID sample with common law $P$, and the displayed supremum is measurable as a function of $S$, the expected Rademacher complexity is\n\n$$\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G):=\\mathbb E_{S\\sim P^m}\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G).$$\n\nThe class may be finite, countable, or uncountable when the indicated supremum is measurable; a specified countable subclass giving the same supremum for every sign vector for almost every sample suffices. This is the one-sided convention: the definition contains no absolute value, and zero need not belong to $\\mathcal G$. Both complexities lie in $[0,1]$: every signed average lies in $[-1,1]$, and for any fixed $g_0\\in\\mathcal G$ the sign-average supremum is at least the sign-average for $g_0$, which is zero.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "For every $w$ in the radius-$B$ ball and every sample point, [F1] gives $|\\langle w,x_i\\rangle|\\le BR\\le1$. Thus $g_w(x_i)\\in[0,1]$, as required for the stated loss class.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "Put $V_\\sigma:=\\sum_i\\sigma_i x_i$. By [F3], $\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)=\\frac1{2m}\\mathbb E_\\sigma[\\sum_i\\sigma_i+\\sup_{\\|w\\|_2\\le B}\\langle w,V_\\sigma\\rangle]$. The sign sum has expectation zero. By [F1], $\\langle w,V_\\sigma\\rangle\\le B\\|V_\\sigma\\|_2$; equality is attained at $w=BV_\\sigma/\\|V_\\sigma\\|_2$ when $V_\\sigma\\ne0$, while both sides are zero when $V_\\sigma=0$. Therefore $\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)=\\frac{B}{2m}\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\|V_\\sigma\\|_2$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "given",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Apply [F2] with $X=\\|V_\\sigma\\|_2$ and $Y=1$ to get $(\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\|V_\\sigma\\|_2)^2\\le\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\|V_\\sigma\\|_2^2$. Expanding the square and using $\\mathbb E_\\sigma[\\sigma_i\\sigma_j]=0$ for $i\\ne j$ and $1$ for $i=j$ gives $\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\|V_\\sigma\\|_2^2=\\sum_i\\|x_i\\|_2^2\\le mR^2$. Substitution in step 1.2 proves $\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)\\le BR/(2\\sqrt m)$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "m,d≥1 and the radius ball contains w=0."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Example and steps 1.2, 2.1: B=0 or R=0 gives complexity 0."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: B=0 or R=0 collapses all sample traces to a singleton."
