          "id": "derivation-3.1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Put $\\mathcal H_B:=\\{h_w:x\\mapsto wx:|w|\\le B\\}$ and $\\mathcal H_0:=\\{h_{-B},h_B\\}\\subseteq\\mathcal H_B$. For every sign vector, with $T=\\sum_i\\sigma_i$, the raw supremum is $\\sup_{|w|\\le B}wT=B|T|$, attained at an endpoint. The clipped supremum is $\\sup_{|w|\\le B}\\operatorname{clip}(w)T=|T|$, also attained at an endpoint because $B\\ge1$. Thus $\\mathcal H_0$ gives both suprema required by [F2]. The map $\\operatorname{clip}$ is 1-Lipschitz: for $u\\le v$, its increase is $0$ if both are below $-1$ or both above $1$, is $v-u$ if both lie in $[-1,1]$, is $v+1\\le v-u$ if $u\\le-1\\le v\\le1$, is $1-u\\le v-u$ if $-1\\le u\\le1\\le v$, and is $2\\le v-u$ if $u\\le-1$ and $v\\ge1$. Thus [F2] applies. The constant offset $1/2$ has zero expected sign sum, and scaling by $1/2$ gives $\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G_B)=\\tfrac12\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\operatorname{clip}\\circ\\mathcal H_B)$. By the general formula in [F2], $\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal H_B)=m^{-1}\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\sup_{|w|\\le B}wT=B\\mathbb E|T|/m\\le B/\\sqrt m$, where [F3] is applied to $X=|T|$ and $Y=1$ and $\\mathbb E T^2=m$. Therefore the contraction route gives $\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G_B)\\le B/(2\\sqrt m)$, which can grow arbitrarily relative to the bound in step 2.1 as $B$ increases.",
